<!--
    A call result is narrowed to its low word, then that derived value feeds a
    comparison inside an inner loop and a store after the loop. The machine
    calls helper once per outer iteration. Marking the call output implied is
    only safe if its whole expression chain reaches one textual sink.

    Pass 1 uses the upstream-faithful option-off form. Pass 2 turns the shipped
    option on explicitly. Both must retain one helper assignment, while the
    existing ghangr-call-return-variable-folding test proves a true single-sink
    call still folds when the option is on. Before the fix pass 2 printed
    helper() at both downstream sinks, so assertion #1 counted only one call
    assignment and assertion #2 counted two duplicated expressions.

    same_block_two_sink covers the cheap same-block case: the masked return
    feeds one store directly and a second store through an add. Its expression
    has only two terminal terms, so the default multiplier budget otherwise
    permits the same call to be printed twice even without a CFG edge.
-->
